[{:seon.task/name "a-value-already-serialized-is-stored-as-it-is"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a written Leaf is serialized twice on Jolt: once in Datahike to hash its content address (``datahike.index.persistent-set/leaf-content-uuid``), once in konserve when the value is stored (the FaslSerializer through ``konserve.impl.defaults/direct-bytes``). Measured through root's writer 2026-11-11 (notes honest-write-phases): the hash-side pass is about 0.8-1.5k ticks a node, 20-35k of a one-datom write's 600k (3-5 percent, inferred from 24 nodes); the store-side serialization (about 120k) is the one that stays. Datahike cannot hand the store the bytes it already made: konserve has no path for a value already serialized. Correct: konserve accepts bytes already in the store's own serialized form for a value (unwrapped for write hooks, refused when the store's serializer is another), and Datahike hashes exactly the bytes that are written (they carry each Datom's cached hash today, so the hashed form and the stored form must be made the same first). Upstream's shape first: read whether upstream konserve or Datahike already has such a path before adding one. Where: the konserve fork and ``datahike.index.persistent-set/store``. Proof when built: a commit serializes each written node once (a counter in a test), same addresses for the same content, a deep `verify-chain` :ok. Should cost: one serialization a node; saves 20-35k ticks of a one-datom write. A format question for the owner, not a small fix: hashing the stored bytes changes every leaf's address (a store made before is read, but no longer passes the deep audit), and konserve gains a path upstream does not have."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-store-key-costs-one-digest"
  :seon.task/plan "Partly done (konserve 1119607, 2026-11-11), and two of this entry's earlier claims were wrong; what is true now. Measured on one setup only (a direct ``d/transact`` of one datom, median of 31, on an LMDB store on disk of 28.0k datoms: 7,000 entities of 4 attributes, history, content addresses, fasl and zstd, branching 64, the :sync writer; 21.3 keys a write; the earlier '171k ticks and 9.4 keys' setup could not be reproduced, its scratch test was deleted): 353.8k ticks and 1.433 MB a write, of which konserve's multi-assoc 190.5k and 785 KB. DONE, each keeping every stored key and byte: (1) no associated data is built for a blob's parts when no encryptor is configured: 348.1k (multi-assoc 183.9k); (3) on Jolt a store key's SHA-512 is one libcrypto call in place of the MessageDigest shim: 335.3k (multi-assoc 170.9k); a uuid key 1654 to 1071 ticks, a keyword 1744 to 1173, a commit-graph vector 2967 to 2428; the same uuid as hasch's for 200 varied keys, and a store written by either code reads with the other. WRONG before, now struck: the meta map beside each value is NOT a constant (each key's holds its own :key, :last-write and :revision), so 'encode it once a call' would change stored bytes and give every key one revision: a format and meaning question for the owner, not a fix; and the store key is not 'one digest': hasch's encoding and uuid5 stay (uuid5 about 500 ticks, the UUID constructor alone 279; the encoding 180 to 300), a vector key is two digests by hasch's format. LEFT, largest first: the MessageDigest shim itself in ``jolt.crypto`` (938 ticks a digest against 353 for one libcrypto call): hasch's commit id, Datahike's per-node address and hasch's nested digests all pay it, so the fix belongs in Jolt, once, not in each caller; the UUID constructor on Jolt (279 ticks); then serialization and zstd, which are the stored bytes' own cost. Not measured: the same through root's writer (27 keys a write; inferred about 16k ticks less). Proof when the shim is fixed: a digest of n bytes costs a stated number of ticks, flat in the count of digests, and this entry's table again."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
]
